WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.768
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.660
RYAN MATSUMOTO: Welcome
to the quick start

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.300
tutorial for Workflows.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.980
In this video,
we're going to walk

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.650
through how to get started with
Workflows, which allows you

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.380
to create serverless workflows
that reliably perform

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.950
a series of related tasks
in an order you define.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.010
To start, make sure you have
a Google Cloud project set up

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.370
in the Google Cloud Platform
Console with billing enabled.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.410
You can either
create a new project

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.479
or use an existing project.

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:34.850
Next, enable the Workflows API.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.720
In the navigation menu,
click on APIs &amp; Services.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.160
Click the Enable APIs and
Services button at the top.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.755
Search for workflows and
select it from the results.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.040
On the Workflows API
page, click Enable

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.060
to enable it for the project.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.430
Setup a service account
to grant permissions

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.120
needed by your workflow
to call services.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.170
In the navigation menu, click on
IAM &amp; Admin, Service Accounts.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:11.400
Click Create Service
Account at the top.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.910
Give your service account
a name and click Create.

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:22.190
Give your service account the
role of Logging, Logs Writer.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:26.520
Click Continue and then Done.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.240
Navigate to the Workflows
page in the Cloud Console.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.925
It will be one of the
options in the Tools section.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.210
On the Workflows
page, click Create.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.795
Provide a name for the workflow,
such as myFirstWorkflow.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.430
Select us-central1 for the
region, and select the service

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.350
account you created earlier
for Service account.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.930
Click Next.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.760
In the Workflow
Editor, we're going

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.650
to use the example workflow.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.990
This workflow fetches
the current date and time

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.090
from a deployed Cloud Function.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.039
It then takes the
current day of the week

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.260
and sends it to
the Wikipedia API

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.410
to fetch relevant articles on
Wikipedia about the current day

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.250
of the week.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:16.805
Click Deploy.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.070
Now that your workflow has
been successfully deployed,

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.140
you can go ahead
and test it out.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.700
After deploying
the workflow, you

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.590
are brought to its Details page.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.740
Click the Execute button at the
top, and then click Execute.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.100
This will execute
your new workflow

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.570
and print out the final
results at the bottom.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.780
You can see that some
Wikipedia articles related

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.900
to today's day of the week
include Thursday Night Football

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.570
and Thursday afternoon.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.560
As you can see, Workflows
allows you to connect APIs

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.970
with serverless computing.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.880
You can also use Workflows to
connect custom services built

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.720
on products like Cloud Functions
and Cloud Run, GCP services,

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.260
such as Cloud Firestore, and any
APIs available on the internet.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.030
And that's it.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.240
Congratulations on creating,
deploying, and executing

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.617
your first workflow.

00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:12.200
Make sure to check out the rest
of our Workflows documentation

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:12.830
to learn more.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

